{:edb/db-before-t 2240 :edb/db-after-t 2241 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535553 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:25:44.527Z" 13194139535553 true] [17592186354979 1100 "Let $v_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $v_2=\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Form $P=(v_1\\ v_2)$ using these columns in this order, with their displayed scales unchanged. For the differential equation $x'(t)=Ax(t)$, define the new coordinates by $u(t)=Dx(t)$ where $D=P^{-1}$. Find the exact matrix $D$." 13194139535553 true] [17592186354979 1100 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" 13194139535553 false] [17592186354979 1145 "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535553 false] [17592186354979 1145 "Use the given eigenvectors as the columns of $P$ in the stated order and with their shown scales:\n\n$$P=(v_1\\ v_2)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-3\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\det P=1\\cdot2-(-3)(-1)=-1.$$\n\nThe requested coordinate map is $u=Dx$ with $D=P^{-1}$. The inverse formula gives\n\n$$D=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&-3\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nBoth $DP$ and $PD$ are the identity matrix, so this is the exact inverse. If $Av_i=\\lambda_i v_i$, then $AP=P\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ and\n\n$$u'=P^{-1}Ax=P^{-1}APu=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)u.$$\n\nThe opposite coordinate direction is $x=Pu$, whose matrix is $P$, not $D$. Fixing column order and eigenvector scales is necessary to specify this exact inverse. For example, $-D$ also yields a decoupled system if one instead uses the rescaled coordinates $\\widetilde u=-u$, but it is not $P^{-1}$ for the specified $P$. The original generic phrase “the decoupling matrix” did not state these conventions. The clarified prompt makes the exact inverse unambiguous; the source accepted this inverse as option c." 13194139535553 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
